Functional Requirements
Phase 1: Configurations
FR1: Rules Selection
Similar to current implementation of rules configuration in app and schema transformation jobs.

The system SHALL present the MSSQL-language dependency rules identified during scanning for user review before transformation begins. Rules selection UI follows the same pattern as the DB schema conversion job:

Apply All Rules — all rules applied (recommended, default)

High Impact Only — only critical/high severity rules applied

Customize — user toggles individual rules on/off

Each rule SHALL display:

Rule ID (sql-java-XXX for Java-MSSQL; sql-csharp-XXX for C#-MSSQL)

Rule Name

Rule Description

Category

Impact level (Critical / High / Moderate / Low)

Summary cards above the rule list:

Rules Applied count

Rules Excluded count

High Impact count

FR2: Dependency Resolution Plan
After rules selection, the system SHALL generate and display a Dependency Resolution Plan for user review and confirmation before transformation begins.

The plan SHALL show:

Total violations found (from Java-MSSQL-dependency.json or CSharp-MSSQL-dependency.json)

Violations in scope (based on selected rules)

Per-violation summary: rule ID, rule name, affected line(s), remediation to be applied

Note — Per-file breakdown is not required. Same UX as M-Basic app modernization applies.

The user reviews the plan and confirms via an "Approve and Start Transformation" CTA. This is a review-and-confirm step — no modification of individual remediations at this stage.

If zero violations are in scope (either zero findings or all rules deselected):

Plan displays: "No violations found in scope. Transformation will apply baseline PostgreSQL compatibility changes only."

User can still proceed.

Phase 2: Transformation
FR3: Right Panel — Progress and Logs Tabs
The right panel SHALL be visible throughout the Transformation phase with two tabs:

Progress tab (default active): Shows overall progress status and milestone-level transformation progress

Logs tab: Shows real-time log stream of transformation activity at file/operation level

The Progress tab SHALL contain two sections:

Section 1 — Overall Progress: A single status banner showing current overall state:

In Progress: "App-DB Dependency Transformation in progress."

Completed: "App-DB Dependency Code Changes Completed Successfully."

Failed: "App-DB Dependency Transformation encountered an error."

Section 2 — Transformation Progress: An expandable card keyed to the application being transformed, showing Application name and Application ID. Below the card header, sequential milestone steps each with a title (bold, coloured when active/complete) and a one-line description, updating in real time:

#

Milestone Title

One-Line Description

1

Initializing Transformation

Loading dependency findings and remediation rules

2

Applying Violation Remediations

Remediating MSSQL-language dependency violations in source files

3

Applying PostgreSQL Compatibility Changes

Updating config, connection drivers, and ORM settings

4

Generating Artifacts

Creating post-transformation report and preparing GitHub commit

5

Transformation Complete

All changes applied successfully

Milestone states: Pending (greyed out, no icon) / In Progress (active colour, spinner icon) / Completed (green checkmark icon) / Failed (red cross icon).

Milestones 1–5 are generic and apply identically across M-Upgrade, M-Basic, and M-Container-Mono pathways. Milestone 3 description remains the same regardless of pathway — the underlying scope differs per pathway (FR4/FR5) but is not surfaced at milestone level.

The Logs tab SHALL display a real-time scrollable log stream. Each log entry SHALL follow this format:



[HH:MM:SS] [LEVEL] Message
Log levels: INFO, WARN, ERROR. Live logs from LLM — similar implementation as schema conversion.

FR4: Code Transformation — All Pathways (Common)
On confirmation, the system SHALL execute the following against the Java or C# source codebase:

Read Java-MSSQL-dependency.json (Java apps) or CSharp-MSSQL-dependency.json (C# apps) findings

For each finding where the corresponding rule is selected:

Look up remediation in the appropriate Remediation KB JSON (java-mssql-remediation-kb.json or csharp-mssql-remediation-kb.json)

Apply remediation to the affected source file(s)

Track change: file modified, rule applied, line(s) changed, remediation ID

Generate change tracking metadata for use in Post-Transformation Report

FR5: Additional Changes — M-Basic Pathway
For M-Basic (cloud readiness) pathway, in addition to violation remediations, the system SHALL apply the following PostgreSQL compatibility changes:

Datasource Configuration:

Update application.properties / application.yml (Java) or appsettings.json / connection string config (C#):

spring.datasource.url → PostgreSQL JDBC URL format jdbc:postgresql://[host]:[port]/[dbname] (Java)

spring.datasource.driver-class-name → org.postgresql.Driver (Java)

Connection string host/port/database → PostgreSQL values with placeholder (C#)

Username / password → placeholder values with inline comment

Replace MSSQL JDBC / ADO.NET driver dependency in pom.xml / build.gradle (Java) or .csproj (C#):

Remove: mssql-jdbc / Microsoft.Data.SqlClient dependency

Add: postgresql JDBC driver (Java) or Npgsql NuGet package (C#) — latest stable version

ORM / Persistence Configuration (if applicable):

Update Hibernate dialect → org.hibernate.dialect.PostgreSQLDialect (Java)

Update persistence.xml or JPA configuration if present (Java)

Update EF Core DbContext → UseNpgsql() in place of UseSqlServer() (C#)

Flag MSSQL-specific ORM annotations or named queries for manual review

Cloud Datasource Compatibility:

If cloud-managed DB service endpoint pattern detected:

AWS RDS: add ?ssl=true&sslmode=require

Azure PostgreSQL: add ?sslmode=require&sslfactory=org.postgresql.ssl.NonValidatingFactory (Java) or SSL Mode=Require in connection string (C#)

GCP Cloud SQL: add ?socketFactory=com.google.cloud.sql.postgres.SocketFactory (Java)

Flag for manual review: "Verify cloud DB endpoint and credentials before deployment"

Connection Pool:

If MSSQL connection pooling detected: replace with HikariCP configuration (Java) or NpgsqlDataSource pooling (C#)

Add HikariCP dependency to pom.xml / build.gradle if not present (Java)

FR6: Additional Changes — M-Container-Mono Pathway
For M-Container-Mono (containerisation) pathway, in addition to violation remediations and all M-Basic datasource changes (FR5), the system SHALL apply the following container-level PostgreSQL compatibility changes:

Dockerfile (if present):

Replace MSSQL JDBC driver COPY / ADD instructions

Add PostgreSQL DB connection environment variable declarations with placeholders:



ENV DB_HOST=<placeholder>
ENV DB_PORT=5432
ENV DB_NAME=<placeholder>
ENV DB_USER=<placeholder>
ENV DB_PASSWORD=<placeholder>
Add inline comments flagging placeholder values for manual replacement

docker-compose.yml (if present):

Update DB service environment variables to PostgreSQL format

Replace MSSQL DB service definition with PostgreSQL service:



db:
  image: postgres:15
  environment:
    POSTGRES_DB: <placeholder>
    POSTGRES_USER: <placeholder>
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD: <placeholder>
  ports:
    - "5432:5432"
Kubernetes Manifests (if present):

Update DB environment variable references to PostgreSQL format using Secret references

Generate sample db-secret.yaml Secret manifest with placeholder PostgreSQL values

Flag for manual review: "Apply db-secret.yaml with actual PostgreSQL credentials before deployment"

Health Check Configuration:

If MSSQL-specific health check SQL detected (e.g., SELECT 1 with MSSQL driver context):

Replace with PostgreSQL-compatible SELECT 1

Flag for manual review

CI/CD Pipeline Files (if detected):

No automated changes applied

Flag MSSQL driver references for manual review

Add inline comment where possible

Phase 3: Artifacts
FR7: Post-Transformation Report
Covered in Feature 5 (post-transformation report spec). Job 3 triggers report generation on transformation completion.

FR8: GitHub Commit
Covered in Feature 6 (GitHub commit spec). Job 3 triggers a separate GitHub commit to target branch on transformation completion.

FR9: Artifacts Phase Completion
Once Post-Transformation Report and GitHub Commit are both complete, Job 3 status updates to "Completed" in the left panel. Job 4 (Validation / Test Artifacts) CTA becomes available.

